title: Why undo and redo do not oscillate
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: Three rules keep undo and redo from trading the same revision back and forth — and make undo, redo, undo compose.

Undo and redo move in opposite directions over the same revisions, so each needs a rule
saying which revisions are ''not'' its own:

* ''A revert of a revert is a redo, not an undo.'' Undo never picks a revert, and redo's third case looks for a revert of an ''ordinary change'' — otherwise pressing redo twice would undo again. A target outside the read window counts as an ordinary change: the conservative reading, which at worst offers a redo with nothing to take back.
* ''Moving forward never lands on an undo.'' What lies ahead of HEAD after a redo-by-rollback is precisely the revert redo just stepped back over; re-applying it would undo, and the two would trade the same revision forever.
* ''An operation is undone only while the revert that undid it still stands.'' A revert that was itself reverted undid nothing any more, and the change it had taken back stands again. So liveness is resolved along the whole revert chain (`reverts_op_id` after `reverts_op_id`), not read off one link. Without it the pair does not compose: after undo then redo, undo would find its target "already undone" and refuse to take it back a second time.
